  "question": {
    "text": "Let a relation $\\mathrm{R}$ on $\\mathrm{N} \\times N$ be defined as: $\\left(x_1, y_1\\right) \\mathrm{R}\\left(x_2, y_2\\right)$ if and only if $x_1 \\leq x_2$ or $y_1 \\leq y_2$. Consider the two statements:\n(I) $\\mathrm{R}$ is reflexive but not symmetric.\n(II) $R$ is transitive Then which one of the following is true?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "nlGHR",
      "text": "Both (I) and (II) are correct.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "dLaVN",
      "text": "Only (II) is correct.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "75aBO",
      "text": "Neither (I) nor (II) is correct.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "XVFYl",
      "text": "Only (I) is correct.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "All $\\left(\\left(\\mathrm{x}_1 \\mathrm{y}_1\\right),\\left(\\mathrm{x}_1, \\mathrm{y}_1\\right)\\right)$ are in $\\mathrm{R}$ where\n$\\mathrm{x}_1, \\mathrm{y}_1 \\in \\mathrm{N} \\therefore \\mathrm{R}$ is reflexive\n$((1,1),(2,3)) \\in \\mathrm{R} \\text { but }((2,3),(1,1)) \\notin \\mathrm{R}$\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{R}$ is not symmetric\n$((2,4),(3,3)) \\in \\mathrm{R}$ and $((3,3),(1,3)) \\in \\mathrm{R}$ but $((2,4)$,\n$(1,3)) \\notin \\mathrm{R}$\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{R}$ is not transitive",
    "image": null
  },
